# Stray SQLite-style artifact created at the repo root by accidental tooling
# (the project's database is Postgres — there is no app-owned brain.db). Ignored
# so it can never be committed by mistake. Anchored to the root only.
/brain.db

# Runtime shim cache for the brain CLI launcher (T1.5).
# `_launcher.ensure_shim()` copies package-data bash templates here on
# first invocation. Dev-checkouts get `<repo>/.shims/`; pipx users get
# `~/.brain/.shims/`. Never tracked.
.shims/

# Runtime pid dir for the brain daemons (watch + build watchers). The fg
# wrappers write `$BRAIN_HOME/run/brain-{watch,build}.pid` here; in a
# dev-checkout $BRAIN_HOME is the repo root, so `run/` lands in the tree.
# Moved off /tmp because macOS's tmp_cleaner reaped the long-lived pid files.
# Never tracked.
run/

# Runtime state (RM-47's restored-from.json, voice-failures.json, watcher-owed.jsonl).
# The CLI writes `$BRAIN_HOME/state/`, and in a dev checkout $BRAIN_HOME is the repo
# root, so `state/` lands in the tree. Local machine state. Never tracked.
state/

# Playwright MCP server scratch dir (browser-automation traces/screenshots/session
# state from MCP browser tool runs). Transient local artifact — never tracked.
.playwright-mcp/

# Brainstorm artifacts (kept local-only — can contain PII / personal references)
.superpowers/

# Agent working reports (implementer reports, code reviews, completion audits,
# CI gate logs). Local-only: they quote raw search output and side-by-side
# retrieval comparisons, which on this corpus contain real names and addresses.
# Untracked-and-unignored is the dangerous state — a single `git add -A` sweeps
# them into a commit. Same failure mode the root-PNG and `docs/audits/*` blocks
# already guard against.
scratchpad/
# Plans / audits / QA — local-only working docs (may contain personal
# references). docs/ is private-by-subdir: the publishable reference docs
# (docs/README.md, docs/cli-reference.md, docs/graphrag.md,
# docs/vault.md, docs/configuration.md), docs/guides/, docs/assets/,
# docs/specs/, docs/plans/, and docs/handoff/ stay trackable; everything below
# is ignored. Add any NEW private subdir/file to this list before it can leak.
#
# docs/handoff/ is SANCTIONED here rather than newly admitted — the enumeration
# above was simply incomplete. Six of its seven files have been tracked since
# `e084c79` (v0.3.0) and `71fa590`; only 2026-08-13-wiki-to-ui-session-handoff.md
# is new, landing in `3b16527`. So the list, not the directory, was the thing out
# of step. Rule 11b names docs/plans/ and docs/specs/ because those are what it
# governs; a session handoff is neither a plan nor a spec, and forcing it into
# one of those directories would misfile it to satisfy a rule that never
# addressed it.
#
# The terms are the same as docs/plans/: PII-scrubbed, synthetic-only, scanned
# before landing. The caveat this note used to carry — that
# 2026-08-13-wiki-to-ui-session-handoff.md was clean of PII *values* but carried
# absolute local worktree paths and another session's operational detail — was
# ACTED ON 2026-08-20, not merely re-recorded: that file now uses <worktree> /
# <main-checkout> placeholders and states in its own header what was changed and
# what was deliberately left (the branch name, which is not machine layout and is
# named in tracked CLAUDE.md anyway).
#
# The CLASS is closed in the working tree as of 2026-08-20: no tracked file
# contains the owner's real home-directory path. Closed by scrubbing the five
# files that still held one — AGENTS.md, both docs/plans/wiki-to-ui files, and
# two tests/test_quartz_* files — to the placeholders the rest of the docs
# already use. What would REOPEN it: any new absolute path pasted out of a real
# shell session.
#
# Re-derive rather than trusting this line, and SCOPE the scan — `/Users/` alone
# is the wrong pattern and never produced the count this note used to cite. Most
# hits are deliberate placeholders (`/Users/you`, `/Users/example`,
# `/Users/<you>`), which are documentation, not exposure:
#
#     git grep -c "/Users/$(basename "$HOME")" -- .    # expect: no output
#
# Note the `-- .` : that scans the WORKING TREE. `git grep … HEAD` answers a
# different question — the committed tree — and keeps reporting the pre-scrub
# state until the scrub is committed. Same reasoning as the docs/audits/ note
# below: a file with no PII can still be a map to something.
#
# docs/specs/ was un-ignored deliberately: CLAUDE.md rule 11b mandates that all
# specs live there, so leaving it ignored meant every design doc in the project
# was one clean checkout from being lost. It was PII-scrubbed first and is now
# synthetic-only, exactly like tests and fixtures — keep it that way (rule 15).
#
# docs/plans/ was un-ignored on the same grounds and the same terms: rule 11b
# mandates that plans live there too, so an ignored plan is one clean checkout
# from gone. Scanned before landing — no PII values, no real corpus identifiers.
#
# docs/audits/ STAYS IGNORED. This is a decision, not an oversight: do NOT
# "finish the job" by moving it up beside docs/plans/. Its files are clean of
# PII *values*, so the usual test passes — and that test is the wrong one here.
# This repository is PUBLIC, and its history still carries real personal data
# that has never been rewritten (removing it would mean rewriting ~446 commits,
# which is destructive and has not been authorised). docs/audits/ holds the PII
# audit, which is a precise index INTO that history: hundreds of findings across
# dozens of files in dozens of named commit SHAs, each labelled by category.
# Committing it would turn an expensive scan into a single `git show`. A file
# that contains no PII can still be a map to PII. The map should keep existing —
# just not inside the repository it maps.
#
# MERGE NOTE (2026-09-02, merging master's PR #8 into this branch): the tree is
# ignored as `docs/audits/*`, not `docs/audits/`, and seven named measurement
# artifacts are allowlisted below. That does not soften the decision above — it
# narrows it. `dir/` makes git refuse to descend, which makes any later
# `!dir/file` unreachable; `dir/*` keeps the same default-deny while leaving one
# escape hatch that must be used ONE FILE AT A TIME, each vetted and each
# carrying its own reason inline. The PII audit is NOT on that list and must
# never be added to it — the paragraph above is why.
# docs/audits/ uses `dir/*` rather than `dir/` so a NAMED file can be
# allowlisted below: git never descends into a directory excluded as `dir/`,
# which makes a later `!dir/file` unreachable. Subdirectories still match `*`,
# so they and their contents stay ignored. Mirrors the
# `tests/eval/baselines/.gitignore` precedent — allowlist explicitly named,
# vetted files ONE AT A TIME; never blanket-unignore this tree.
docs/audits/*
# Wave 0 of the agentic-token-reduction plan: the committed before/after
# payload baseline every later wave diffs against. Synthetic/topical queries
# only, PII-free by construction (see scripts/token_payload_queries.txt).
!docs/audits/2026-08-10-token-payload-baseline.json
# Wave 1 of the same plan: the "after" measurement the −57.4% `search_brief`
# saving is computed from, diffed against the Wave-0 baseline above. Same
# construction, same synthetic query set, so PII-free on the same grounds —
# it carries only counts, the retrieval config block, and the query strings.
!docs/audits/2026-08-11-token-payload-after-wave1.json
# Wave 2 of the same plan: the provenance behind the 183,940 / 8,998 / −95.1%
# routing figures published in skills/consult-brain and docs/agent-skills.md,
# plus the corpus statistics behind the 18,218-char mean body and the Krisp
# transcript distribution. PII-free by construction on the same grounds as the
# two above — five generic topical queries, and otherwise only counts, the
# retrieval config, and the command that produced them. No titles, no bodies,
# no participants.
!docs/audits/2026-08-11-wave2-routing-counterfactual.md
!docs/audits/2026-08-11-wave2-routing-counterfactual.json
# Wave 3 of the same plan: the delivered-payload measurement the ceilings are
# proved against — the plan's "How we prove it worked" steps 3 and 6 refuse to
# accept a ceiling number with no measurement beside it. Same synthetic query
# set and same construction as the three above, so PII-free on the same
# grounds. Read-only run against prod (the script is read-only by construction
# and sets a server-enforced read-only transaction).
#
# NOTE when diffing this against the Wave-0/1 files: its `recall` rows are
# 8 tokens/query higher BY DESIGN — Wave 3 added the additive `payload_tokens`
# key to the recall payload and `measure_recall` mirrors it. That +88 total is
# the key, not a regression. `search` and `search_brief` are TOKEN-identical to
# Wave 1 (0 delta on all 11 queries) — not byte-identical: their `chars` move
# -2..+2 per query. With BRAIN_RECENCY_HALFLIFE_DAYS set, each `score` carries
# a now()-derived decay term, so runs days apart legitimately serialize
# different bytes at identical token counts (see `measure_search`'s docstring).
!docs/audits/2026-08-13-token-payload-after-wave3.json
# Wave 4 — the adaptive snippet cut's ENGAGEMENT measurement, produced by
# `scripts/token_payload_report.py --adaptive-stats --json` against the live
# corpus (read-only). Committed because it is the evidence the wave is judged
# on, and it does NOT support the wave: the Otsu path engages on 41/55 results
# (74.5%, comfortably above the plan's ~20% revert trigger) yet the DELIVERED
# token delta is 0 on all 55, because only 3/55 results admit any neighbour at
# the shipped 200-token budget and 47/55 have a matched chunk that alone reaches
# the 1600-char cap. The measured "3 of 5 pinned at the cap" defect is caused
# by the MATCHED CHUNK, which neighbour admission cannot address.
#
# CAUTION when reading this artifact: its `results_matched_chunk_fills_cap`
# reads 55, under the SUPERSEDED definition of that key (it counted rows whose
# matched-only tokens equalled the delivered tokens — "the expansion added
# nothing" — which is indeed 55/55). It is NOT the 47/55 stated above; this
# same artifact records that correctly as `legacy_pinned`/`adaptive_pinned: 47`.
# The successor artifact reuses the key name under the literal definition
# (`len(matched_chunk) >= cap`) and reports 47. Cite the 47.
#
# Deliberately NOT named `*token-payload*.json`: that glob is the derivation
# set for `DEFAULT_RECALL_MAX_BUDGET_TOKENS` in
# `tests/test_config_mcp_ceilings.py`, and neither artifact has `recall` rows.
!docs/audits/2026-08-13-adaptive-snippet-engagement.json
# The successor measurement, from `--snippet-constraints`. The artifact above
# is the historical record of the rejected mechanism and is NO LONGER
# REPRODUCIBLE (the mechanism it measured was removed); this one IS, and it
# measures the two constraints that were found to actually bind: the walk
# budget (3/55 results admit any neighbour) and the char cap (47/55 have a
# matched chunk at or over it, and 11,514 of 30,727 produced tokens are
# discarded unread). Both are kept: the first is the evidence for the decision,
# the second is what a future wave would re-run.
!docs/audits/2026-08-13-snippet-constraints.json
# The F6 draft/sensitivity residue audit — prose moved VERBATIM out of
# CLAUDE.md (already checked in) when CLAUDE.md was slimmed under the
# instruction-size limit (docs/plans/2026-10-29-slim-claude-md.md). It is a
# standing review checkpoint CLAUDE.md points at, and contains no corpus data.
!docs/audits/f6-residue-audit.md
