# Real PII-gate denylist (real names/customers/codenames) — keep local (rule 15).
# The checked-in file is .pii-denylist.local.txt.example (synthetic).
#
# What the gate's EMAIL check actually permits, because it is narrower than the
# repo has claimed elsewhere: `scripts/hooks/pre-commit` allows an address only
# if its host ends in one of a hardcoded alternation — re-derive it with
# `grep -n 'emails=' -A2 scripts/hooks/pre-commit`. That set is NOT "the RFC 2606
# reserved names", which is how at least one commit message describes it. RFC 2606
# reserves the `.test/.example/.invalid/.localhost` TLDs and `example.{com,net,org}`
# — and nothing else. The alternation also admits `acme`, `contoso` and `northwind`
# under `.com/.org/.net`, plus `test.` and `invalid.` under them. Those are
# conventional synthetic-company placeholders, not reserved names, and `acme.com`,
# `contoso.com` and `northwind.com` are real registered domains.
#
# This is deliberate and worth keeping — the placeholders are what the fixtures
# actually use — but it should be described accurately, because "only reserved
# names" is the kind of claim someone leans on when deciding a fixture is safe.
# The gate scans the STAGED DIFF, not the tree, so existing tracked uses are
# grandfathered and a green commit is not evidence the tree is clean.
/.pii-denylist.local.txt

# Generated audio-overview artifacts (brain audio) — real content, never commit
audio/

# Ad-hoc screenshots dropped at the repo root during UI/design work (e.g.
# `w5-1440-light.png` from the wave-5 web-UI pass). Root-level only, so
# committed assets under docs/ or src/ are unaffected. Kept out of history
# because they are one-off working artifacts, not deliverables — and because a
# stray root PNG is exactly what a `git add -A` sweeps into a release commit.
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg

